## ch-elem | Elements with stories | chem | 0 | - | ch-org
story 4 | Phosphorus was discovered in urine
- 1669: alchemist Hennig Brand boiled down large quantities of urine looking for the philosopher's stone.
- He got a glowing substance instead: phosphorus, the first element discovered by a known person.
- *It glows because it slowly reacts with oxygen in the air.*
> Gallium melts in your hand

explain 3 | Gallium melts at about 30°C
- Hold a spoon of it and it melts into a silvery puddle.
- A classic prank: a gallium spoon that "disappears" when stirring hot tea.
- *It also makes aluminium crumble by seeping between its grains.*
> Element names from places

quiz 3 | Four elements are named after the same Swedish village. Which?
* Ytterby (yttrium, ytterbium, terbium, erbium)
o Uppsala
o Stockholm
o Lund
= A mine near Ytterby yielded several rare earth minerals.
> The most expensive element

explain 4 | Helium is running out (sort of)
- Helium is so light that once released, it escapes Earth's gravity into space.
- Most is extracted from natural gas, where it's trapped underground.
- *MRI machines need liquid helium. Party balloons waste it.*
> Next: more chemistry everyday

## ch-day | Everyday chemistry 2 | chem | 0 | - | ch-elem
explain 3 | Why cut apples go brown
- Cutting releases an enzyme (polyphenol oxidase) that reacts phenols with oxygen, forming brown pigments.
- Lemon juice slows it by lowering the pH and acting as an antioxidant.
- *Same reaction browns avocados and bananas.*
> Why bread rises

explain 3 | Yeast farts make bread rise
- Yeast ferments sugars into carbon dioxide and a little alcohol.
- Gluten traps the gas in bubbles.
- *The alcohol bakes off; the bubbles set into the crumb.*
> Rust is slow fire

explain 3 | Rust is slow burning
- Iron combining with oxygen (and water) to form iron oxides.
- Burning is the same kind of reaction, much faster.
- *Hand warmers use powdered iron rusting quickly to release heat.*
> Why the sea doesn't freeze easily

quiz 3 | Why does stainless steel resist rust?
* Chromium forms a thin protective oxide layer that heals itself
o It contains no iron
o It's coated with paint
o It's too hard to react
= At least about 10.5% chromium. Scratch it and the layer re-forms.
> Next: ecology

## bi-eco | Ecology | bio | 0 | - | bo-evo
story 5 | Wolves changed Yellowstone's rivers (partly)
- Wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone in 1995 after 70 years' absence.
- Elk numbers fell and their behaviour changed, letting some streamside willows and aspens recover.
- *The popular "wolves changed the rivers" story is debated by ecologists, but trophic cascades are real.*
> Keystone species

explain 4 | Keystone species
- Robert Paine removed starfish from a tidal pool in the 1960s. Mussels took over and diversity crashed.
- Some species have outsized effects on their ecosystem.
- *Sea otters, which eat urchins that eat kelp, protect kelp forests.*
> Insects are declining

explain 4 | The insect decline
- A 2017 study of German nature reserves found flying insect biomass fell by over 75% in 27 years.
- Causes may include habitat loss, pesticides and climate change.
- *Insects pollinate crops and feed birds: losses ripple outward.*
> Most species are unknown

quiz 3 | Roughly what fraction of Earth's species have been formally described?
* Perhaps 15–20% (estimates vary widely)
o About 90%
o About 50%
o About 1%
= Around 2 million species are named; estimates of the total range from about 8 million to far more.
> Next: origins of life

## bi-orig | The origin of life | bio | 0 | - | bi-eco
story 4 | Miller–Urey made amino acids from scratch
- 1952: Stanley Miller passed sparks through a flask of water, methane, ammonia and hydrogen.
- After a week, the water contained amino acids, building blocks of proteins.
- *Reanalysis of his sealed samples decades later found over 20 amino acids.*
> The RNA world

explain 4 | The RNA world hypothesis
- RNA can both store information (like DNA) and catalyse reactions (like proteins).
- So early life may have run on RNA alone.
- *Your ribosomes, which build proteins, are themselves catalytic RNA at their core.*
> Life appeared fast

explain 4 | Life appeared early
- Earth formed about 4.5 billion years ago.
- Evidence of life goes back at least 3.5 billion years, possibly earlier.
- *Complex multicellular life took around 3 billion years longer.*
> Next: plants

## bi-plant | Plants | bio | 0 | - | bi-orig
explain 3 | Plants are mostly made of air
- A tree's mass comes overwhelmingly from carbon dioxide in the air, not from the soil.
- Photosynthesis turns CO₂ and water into sugars, using light.
- *Van Helmont's 17th-century willow experiment found the soil barely lost weight.*
> Trees talk underground (sort of)

explain 4 | The "wood wide web"
- Fungal networks connect tree roots, trading sugars for nutrients.
- Some studies suggest resources pass between trees through them.
- *How much trees "share" is debated; some popular claims go beyond the evidence.*
> The oldest living things

quiz 3 | A bristlecone pine in California is about how old?
* About 4,800 years
o About 480 years
o About 48,000 years
o About 1,000 years
= "Methuselah", in the White Mountains. Its exact location is kept secret.
> Plants can count

explain 4 | Venus flytraps count
- A flytrap snaps shut only after its trigger hairs are touched twice within about 20 seconds.
- It starts digesting only after more touches: confirmation the prey is real and struggling.
- *Counting to five, without a nervous system.*
> Next: physics oddities

## ph-odd | Physics oddities | physics | 0 | - | ev-phys
explain 4 | Hot water can freeze faster than cold (sometimes)
- The Mpemba effect, named after a Tanzanian student who noticed it in 1963 making ice cream.
- Evaporation, convection and dissolved gases may play roles.
- *Whether it's real in carefully controlled experiments is still debated.*
> Glass breaks at the speed of sound in glass

explain 4 | Prince Rupert's drops
- Drip molten glass into cold water and you get a tadpole-shaped drop.
- The head survives hammer blows; snip the tail and the whole thing explodes into powder.
- *Internal stresses: the surface is in compression, the core in tension.*
> Non-Newtonian fluids

explain 3 | Walking on custard
- A cornflour and water mix thickens under sudden force: you can run across a pool of it, but sink if you stand still.
- It's a shear-thickening, non-Newtonian fluid.
- *Ketchup does the opposite: it thins when shaken.*
> Superfluid helium climbs walls

explain 5 | Superfluid helium
- Below about 2.17 K, helium-4 becomes a superfluid with zero viscosity.
- It creeps up container walls as a thin film and drips off the bottom.
- *A quantum effect visible to the naked eye.*
> Next: arts again

## ar-mus2 | Music history and theory | arts | 0 | - | ar-mus
explain 3 | Why a minor chord sounds sad (in Western music)
- Major and minor chords differ by one note, lowered by a semitone.
- The "sad" association is largely cultural and learned, though acoustic factors are debated.
- *Many cultures don't map minor = sad.*
> Mozart wrote a symphony at 8

story 4 | Mozart transcribed a secret piece from memory at 14
- Allegri's Miserere was sung only in the Sistine Chapel, and copying it was forbidden.
- In 1770 the 14-year-old Mozart reportedly heard it and wrote it out from memory.
- *Musicologists note the piece is partly repetitive, but the feat is still remarkable.*
> The Rite of Spring riot

story 4 | The Rite of Spring caused a near-riot in 1913
- Stravinsky's ballet premiered in Paris with jarring rhythms and dissonance.
- The audience jeered and argued loudly enough to drown out the orchestra.
- *Within a few years it was hailed as a masterpiece.*
> The Beatles couldn't read music

myth 3 T | None of the Beatles could read or write standard musical notation.
= They composed by ear and recorded, working with producer George Martin on arrangements.
= Paul McCartney has said this himself.
> Next: art again

## ar-art2 | Artists and their works | arts | 0 | - | ar-art
story 4 | The Scream has a faint pencil note
- Edvard Munch's 1893 painted version has a tiny inscription: "Can only have been painted by a madman."
- Infrared analysis in 2021 confirmed it was Munch's own handwriting.
- *He likely wrote it after critics called him mentally ill.*
> Vermeer used optics (maybe)

explain 4 | Did Vermeer use a camera obscura?
- His paintings show optical effects like soft, out-of-focus highlights.
- Some argue he traced projections from a camera obscura; others say he simply observed carefully.
- *There's no documentary proof either way.*
> Frida Kahlo painted from bed

story 4 | Frida Kahlo painted from her bed
- After a bus accident at 18 left her badly injured, she painted with a mirror above her bed.
- About a third of her works are self-portraits.
- *"I paint myself because I am so often alone and because I am the subject I know best."*
> Banksy's shredded painting

story 4 | The painting that shredded itself at auction
- 2018: moments after Banksy's Girl with Balloon sold for over £1 million at Sotheby's, a hidden shredder in the frame cut through it.
- It was renamed "Love is in the Bin".
- *In 2021 it resold for about £18.6 million.*
> Arts track part two complete
